// Concept texts, batch 6 (names HARLEQUIN ICHTHYOSIS to JEALOUS).
// Row: [name, group code, short, feels, looks, misreading, help]. An empty help text uses the group's default.
// Group codes: P phobia, A anxiety, M mood, D disorder, T thinking pattern, C control or manipulation tactic,
// R trait or relationship pattern, V harmful habit, U strength or virtue, K practice or tool,
// F fascination or urge, B body or medical.
export default [
  ["HARLEQUIN ICHTHYOSIS", "B", "A rare inherited skin condition that causes thick, plate-like skin that can crack.", "Tight, cracked skin can be painful and can make movement and eating harder. Daily care can feel like a constant task.", "Regular skin care routines, frequent medical visits, and special support for dressing, eating, or moving.", "It is inherited, not caused by poor hygiene, and it is not contagious. Specialist skin care can ease symptoms, prevent infections, and support daily life.", ""],
  ["HARMONIOUS", "U", "A sense of inner and outer parts working together without constant conflict.", "Life can feel smoother and calmer when your choices and feelings point the same way. Small conflicts may feel easier to manage.", "A steady tone in conversation, fair handling of disagreements, and balanced daily routines.", "Harmony is not the absence of problems or conflict. It is the ability to face difficulties without losing your center.", ""],
  ["HARMONIOUS ENERGY ALIGNMENT", "U", "A sense that your thoughts, feelings and actions point in the same direction.", "Decisions can feel clearer when head and heart agree. Inner friction may fade for a while, which brings relief.", "Consistent choices, calm follow-through on plans, and openness about what matters most.", "Alignment is not a fixed state, and it is not a sign of being above struggle. It is returned to again and again as life changes.", ""],
  ["HARMONIOUS INNER BALANCE", "U", "A steady internal balance between rest, effort, feeling and thought.", "Balance can feel grounded and unhurried. It can ease the urge to swing between overworking and collapsing.", "Regular breaks, steady moods, and a pace that stays sustainable across the week.", "Balance does not mean equal time for everything every day. It means noticing when one part of life needs more care.", ""],
  ["HARMONIOUS LIFE INTEGRATION", "U", "A sense that work, relationships, rest and values fit together in one life.", "Life can feel whole rather than split into separate pieces. Choices can feel less like trade-offs between competing needs.", "Keeping commitments at work and home, and making room for rest and close people.", "Integration does not mean every day is perfectly balanced. It means the parts of life support one another over time.", ""],
  ["HARMONIOUS SELF-ACCEPTANCE", "U", "A calm acceptance of yourself, including the parts you would like to change.", "Self-acceptance can bring a softer inner voice and less fear of being seen. Growth can feel possible instead of threatening.", "Speaking about yourself with honesty and without harsh put-downs, and accepting feedback calmly.", "Accepting yourself does not mean giving up on change. It is the base that makes change feel safer and more steady.", ""],
  ["HARMONIOUS SELF-INTEGRATION", "U", "Bringing different parts of your own character into one coherent sense of self.", "Parts of you that once felt in conflict can start to feel like one person. The change can feel steady and quietly relieving.", "Being the same person across settings, and speaking and acting in line with your values.", "Integration does not erase the past or remove all inner tension. It means the different parts can coexist with less struggle.", ""],
  ["HARMONIOUS SELF-RELATIONSHIP", "U", "A kind, fair and steady relationship with yourself over time.", "Your inner voice can feel more like a supportive friend than a judge. Mistakes can be faced without a flood of shame.", "Patience with yourself after errors, honest self-talk, and keeping promises you make to yourself.", "A healthy relationship with yourself is not constant praise. It includes honesty, limits and forgiveness when you slip.", ""],
  ["HEADACHES", "B", "Pain or pressure in the head that can be mild, sharp, throbbing or constant.", "Pain can make it hard to focus, rest or enjoy the day. Some people feel irritable or tired when headaches keep coming back.", "Rubbing the head, lying down in a dark room, missing work or school, or taking pain medicine often.", "Most headaches are not dangerous, but frequent or severe ones need a doctor's review. A sudden, very severe headache needs emergency care right away.", ""],
  ["HEALING CELEBRATION", "U", "Marking a step forward in healing with a small, meaningful act of recognition.", "Pausing to notice progress can bring pride, relief and a little tenderness. Small wins can feel bigger when they are named.", "Writing down a milestone, sharing it with a supporter, or treating yourself to something kind.", "A celebration does not need to be big or public. It also does not mean the healing is finished.", ""],
  ["HEALTH ANXIETY", "A", "Persistent worry about being seriously ill, even when medical checks are reassuring.", "Body sensations can set off fear quickly. Reassurance may bring brief relief, and then the worry returns.", "Repeated checking of symptoms online, frequent doctor visits, or asking others for reassurance often.", "The worry is real to the person, even when the cause is not serious. Therapy can help break the cycle of checking and fear.", ""],
  ["HEART PALPITATIONS", "B", "Feeling your heartbeat as fast, pounding, fluttering or skipping in the chest.", "The heartbeat can feel alarming, even when it passes quickly. Fear can make the sensation feel bigger.", "Touching the chest, pausing mid-task, or sitting down suddenly when the heart feels unusual.", "Palpitations are often harmless and linked to stress, caffeine or poor sleep. Chest pain, fainting or trouble breathing need urgent medical care.", ""],
  ["HEART WISDOM", "U", "A deep, caring kind of knowing that comes from listening to what matters to you.", "Heart wisdom can feel calm and warm. It can help you sense what is right even when logic is unclear.", "Choosing kindness in a hard moment, or pausing to ask what matters before deciding.", "Heart wisdom does not replace facts or advice. It works best when it is combined with honest thinking.", ""],
  ["HEART-CENTERED CONNECTION", "U", "Close bonds built on warmth, honesty and genuine care for one another.", "Connection can feel safe and open. It can make hard days easier to face with someone beside you.", "Listening without rushing, sharing feelings honestly, and checking in on one another.", "Heart-centered connection is not perfect closeness with no conflict. Repair after a disagreement is part of it.", ""],
  ["HEART-LED DECISIONS", "K", "Making choices guided by your values and feelings, along with facts and advice.", "Choices can feel more honest and personal. Doubt may still come, and it helps to slow down before deciding.", "Pausing before big decisions, asking what matters most, and explaining choices to others.", "Following your heart does not mean ignoring practical facts. The best decisions use both feeling and clear thinking.", ""],
  ["HEART-OPEN BELONGING", "U", "Feeling welcome in a group while staying open to people who are different.", "Belonging can feel warm and steadying. Being open to others can also bring a sense of shared humanity.", "Greeting newcomers, inviting others in, and listening to views that differ from your own.", "Being open does not mean accepting harm or giving up your boundaries. Belonging works best when it is mutual and respectful.", ""],
  ["HEARTFUL RELATING", "U", "Relating to others with warmth, attention and sincere care for their experience.", "Warm contact can feel rewarding and open. It can soften tension and make people feel seen.", "Asking how someone is doing, listening with full attention, and responding with kindness.", "Heartful relating is not agreeing with everything. It means staying kind while being honest about your own needs.", ""],
  ["HEBEPHILIA", "F", "A sexual interest in children who are going through puberty.", "Feelings like this can bring shame, fear and confusion. They can be very distressing to carry alone.", "Usually private, with secrecy, shame and fear of asking for help.", "Having these feelings is not the same as acting on them, and getting help early protects children. Any sexual act with a child is abuse, and viewing sexual images of children harms real children.", "Sexual interest in children is serious, and acting on it harms children. Stop It Now offers a free, confidential helpline for people worried about their own thoughts or behavior, or someone else's: 1-888-773-8368. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis, or call 911 if someone is in danger."],
  ["HEDONOMANIAC", "F", "A strong, constant drive to chase pleasure, comfort and excitement.", "Pleasure can feel urgent and hard to stop. Once it fades, a restless need for the next thrill can return.", "Frequent indulgence, impulsive spending, and trouble saying no to fun.", "Enjoying pleasure is healthy, and it is not a moral failing. The pattern matters when it causes harm, debt or lost sleep.", ""],
  ["HELIOPHILE", "F", "A love of the sun and warm sunlight.", "Sunlight can feel warm, bright and uplifting. Time in it may bring a lift in mood and a sense of being alive.", "Seeking sunny spots, spending time outdoors, and planning activities around clear, warm days.", "Loving the sun is healthy in moderation. Sun exposure still needs protection, such as shade, hats and sunscreen, to prevent burns.", ""],
  ["HELPLESSNESS", "M", "A feeling that nothing you do will change things, even when you try.", "Effort can feel pointless, and small steps may seem unimportant. Exhaustion and frustration can build quickly.", "Giving up on tasks early, saying that nothing will work, or not asking for help.", "Helplessness often comes with stress, depression or past harm. It is not a fixed truth about your life, and support and treatment can help change it.", "If helplessness brings thoughts of ending your life, call or text 988 in the US now, and call 911 if you are in danger."],
  ["HEMATOPHILE", "F", "A strong fascination with blood, including, for some people, a sexual interest.", "The pull toward blood can feel intense and mixed with fear, curiosity or shame. It can be hard to explain to others.", "Keeping close attention on blood, or talking about it often.", "Being drawn to blood is not the same as wanting to hurt anyone. Acting on any interest without clear consent, or in ways that harm others, is serious harm.", "A therapist who works with sexual health can help you talk it through. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis. If someone is in danger, call 911."],
  ["HEMISPATIAL NEGLECT", "B", "A brain condition in which one side of space is ignored or not noticed.", "The person may not realize one side is missing. Others may feel worried or confused by the gaps in attention.", "Bumping into things on one side, missing words or objects on one side, or turning only to one side.", "It is not a choice, and the person is not being careless or rude. It often follows a stroke or brain injury and needs medical care.", ""],
  ["HEMOPHILIA", "B", "An inherited bleeding disorder in which blood does not clot well.", "Bleeding can last longer than expected, and injuries can feel frightening. Joint pain and worry about bleeding are common.", "Bruises that appear easily, bleeding after dental work or injury, or sore and swollen joints.", "It is not caused by poor care or a lack of effort. Modern treatment can prevent many serious bleeds when care is ongoing.", ""],
  ["HEMOPHOBIA", "P", "A strong fear of blood, injury, needles or medical procedures.", "Seeing blood can bring a rush of fear, dizziness or faintness. The body may suddenly feel weak or cold.", "Turning away, leaving the room, avoiding medical visits, or fainting at the sight of blood or needles.", "It is a real and treatable fear, not a weakness. Gradual exposure, with a trained therapist, can help many people feel more at ease.", ""],
  ["HICCUP REFLEX", "B", "A sudden, repeated spasm of the diaphragm that makes a hiccup sound.", "Hiccups can be annoying and can interrupt speech or sleep. They usually pass on their own.", "Short, sharp sounds from the chest and throat, sometimes repeated again and again.", "Hiccups are harmless in most cases. Hiccups that last for days need a doctor's review.", ""],
  ["HIEROPHILIA", "F", "A fascination with sacred objects, places, rituals or holy people.", "Sacred places can bring awe, peace or a sense of belonging. Some people feel deeply moved by ritual and tradition.", "Visiting holy sites often, collecting religious objects, or studying rituals with great devotion.", "Caring about sacred things is part of many faiths and traditions. It becomes a concern only when it brings distress or harms daily life.", ""],
  ["HISTRIONIC", "R", "Dramatic, attention-seeking emotion and behavior, often aimed at being noticed.", "Being noticed can feel vital, and quiet times may feel empty or flat. Emotions can rise and fall quickly.", "Dramatic speech, quick shifts in mood, and a strong need for attention or praise.", "Expressive people are not all histrionic. The pattern matters when attention-seeking is constant and shapes most relationships.", ""],
  ["HISTRIONIC PERSONALITY DISORDER", "D", "A long-running pattern of intense, attention-seeking emotion that causes strain.", "Feelings can shift quickly and feel very strong. Being overlooked can feel painful and hard to tolerate.", "Dramatic displays, quick mood swings, and seeking constant reassurance or center stage.", "It is not a choice to be dramatic, and it is not simply a flaw of character. Therapy can help build steadier emotions and more balanced relationships.", ""],
  ["HIVES", "B", "Raised, itchy welts on the skin that come and go within hours.", "Itching and burning can be uncomfortable and can make sleep hard. The welts can appear in new places without warning.", "Red or pale welts that move around the body, scratching, and sudden flares after foods, stress or heat.", "Hives are often caused by allergies, infections or stress, and many have no clear cause. Swelling of the lips or throat, or trouble breathing, needs emergency care right away.", ""],
  ["HOARDING", "V", "A habit of keeping items, even when they are no longer useful or needed.", "Letting go can feel painful or unsafe, even when the space is crowded. Keeping things can bring short comfort and a sense of control.", "Piles of items in rooms, reluctance to let others clean, and stress when asked to discard things.", "Keeping things is common and not a moral failing. It becomes harmful when clutter blocks daily life, safety or relationships.", ""],
  ["HOARDING DISORDER", "D", "A lasting difficulty discarding possessions, which crowds living space and causes distress.", "Throwing things away can feel frightening, even when clutter is causing problems. Strong urges to save items can return again and again.", "Crowded rooms, clutter near doors or stairs, and avoiding visitors or repairs in the home.", "It is not laziness or dirtiness. It is a recognized, treatable condition, and therapy that targets the urge to save and sort can help.", ""],
  ["HODOPHOBE", "P", "A strong fear of traveling, especially on roads or long journeys.", "Thinking about a trip can bring dread, a racing heart and a wish to stay home. Fear can grow with each missed trip.", "Avoiding trips, canceling plans, or needing a lot of reassurance before leaving home.", "It is a real fear that often responds well to gradual exposure. Travel can become possible step by step, with support.", ""],
  ["HOOVERER", "R", "A person who often pulls someone back after a break, using warmth or promises.", "The person being pulled back may feel hopeful, guilty and unsure what is real. Each return can bring relief that fades quickly.", "Sudden warmth after silence, promises to change, and distance again soon after.", "Not every return is a tactic, and people can change. The pattern matters when comebacks keep a cycle of hurt going.", ""],
  ["HOOVERING", "C", "A tactic of pulling someone back into contact after distance, with warmth or apologies.", "Warm contact can bring relief and hope, which can make earlier hurt harder to see. The cycle can leave a person confused about what really happened.", "Sudden messages after silence, big apologies, and withdrawal again once the moment passes.", "A single kind message is not proof of a tactic. The pattern is repeated cycles of closeness and hurt, which can be noted and talked through.", ""],
  ["HOOVEROPHILE", "C", "A strong drive to pull people back into contact after a break, often to keep control.", "Each comeback can bring hope and confusion, which can keep someone attached. Being pulled back and pushed away can feel exhausting.", "Repeated returns after silence and warm contact that fades again.", "Wanting to reconnect is human, and not every return is a tactic. The pattern is repeated cycles that leave someone stuck and hurt.", ""],
  ["HOPE", "M", "A sense that things can improve, which makes effort and planning feel possible.", "Hope can bring a lighter feeling and a sense of direction. It may come and go, even when life is hard.", "Making plans for the future, trying new steps, or talking about what might change.", "Hope is not denial of problems. It is a realistic belief that change is possible, even if progress is slow.", ""],
  ["HOPEFUL", "M", "Feeling that good things are possible, even if they are not certain.", "Hopefulness can feel open and light. It can make it easier to try again after a setback.", "Speaking about the future with interest, taking small steps, and asking what might help.", "Being hopeful is not the same as being naive. It can sit alongside honest worry about real risks.", ""],
  ["HOPELESSNESS", "M", "A deep sense that nothing will get better and that effort is pointless.", "The future can feel closed and heavy, and tasks can seem out of reach. Hope can feel impossible to find.", "Withdrawing from people, giving up on plans, or saying that nothing matters or will change.", "Hopelessness is a common part of depression, stress or crisis. It is not a true reading of your future, and it can change with care.", "If you have thoughts of ending your life, call or text 988 in the US now, and call 911 if you are in danger."],
  ["HOPLOMANIAC", "F", "A strong fascination with weapons, such as guns, swords or knives.", "Weapons can feel thrilling, powerful or frightening. Some people feel drawn to their design and history.", "Collecting or studying weapons, talking about them often, or spending a lot of time on related hobbies.", "Interest in history, sport or craft is common and not harmful by itself. It becomes a concern when it leads to fear, conflict or harm.", ""],
  ["HORMONAL IMBALANCES", "B", "Changes in hormone levels that can affect mood, sleep, energy and cycles.", "Symptoms can feel confusing, since they may come and go. Mood swings or tiredness can make daily life harder.", "Changes in sleep, energy, mood or menstrual cycles, and new questions about health.", "Hormone changes are common and often treatable. A doctor can test levels and suggest a plan that fits you.", ""],
  ["HUMBLE", "U", "Seeing your strengths and limits honestly, without needing to be the center of attention.", "Humility can bring calm and openness. It can make it easier to learn from others and to admit mistakes.", "Asking for feedback, thanking others openly, and admitting when you do not know something.", "Humility is not self-criticism or low self-worth. It means seeing yourself clearly, with respect for yourself and others.", ""],
  ["HUMILITY", "U", "An honest view of yourself that makes room to learn from others.", "Humility can feel steadying and free. You do not have to pretend to know everything.", "Listening closely, saying 'I was wrong', and sharing credit with others.", "Humility is not weakness or self-erasure. It combines an honest view of your gifts with openness to what others can teach you.", ""],
  ["HUMOR", "U", "A gift for noticing and sharing what is funny, especially in hard times.", "Humor can lighten a heavy moment and bring people closer. It can also bring relief when life feels too serious.", "Sharing a warm joke, laughing with others, or finding the lighter side of a tense situation.", "Humor is not the same as dismissing pain. It is healthiest when it brings people together rather than hiding the truth.", ""],
  ["HUNTINGTON'S DISEASE", "B", "An inherited brain disease that slowly affects movement, thinking and mood.", "Families may feel fear, grief and uncertainty as symptoms change over time. Planning and support can bring some steadiness.", "Jerky or uncontrolled movements, slower thinking, mood changes, and needing more help with daily tasks.", "It is not caused by lifestyle, stress or a single mistake. Care teams can help with movement, mood, safety and daily life.", ""],
  ["HUTCHINSON-GILFORD PROGERIA SYNDROME", "B", "A rare genetic condition that causes children to age faster than usual.", "Families may feel deep grief and hard choices, along with love and a wish to make each day count. Care can be emotionally heavy.", "Slow growth, hair loss, and early signs of aging, often noticed in the first years of life.", "It is not caused by anything a parent did. Specialist care can ease symptoms and support the child and family.", ""],
  ["HYPER-INDEPENDENCE", "R", "Refusing to rely on others, even when help is needed and would be welcome.", "Needing help can feel exposing or unsafe. Doing everything alone may bring pride, but also loneliness and strain.", "Doing everything alone, turning down offers of support, and brushing off concern from others.", "Self-reliance is valuable. The pattern is when it blocks closeness, rest or the care you need.", ""],
  ["HYPERAROUSAL", "M", "A state of heightened alarm in the body, with tension and trouble settling.", "The body can feel on edge, and rest may feel impossible. Small sounds or changes can feel startling.", "Jumpiness, restlessness, trouble sitting still, and quick reactions to noises or sudden movement.", "It is the body's alarm system responding to stress or past threat. It can ease with rest, breathing practice and support.", ""],
  ["HYPERAROUSAL REGULATION", "K", "A practice of calming an alarmed body and mind, one step at a time.", "Slowing down can bring relief and a sense of control over the body's alarm. Progress often comes in small steps.", "Slow breathing, feeling the feet on the floor, a short walk, or a glass of cool water.", "Regulation does not erase stress. It helps the body return to a calmer baseline so you can think and act more clearly.", ""],
  ["HYPERAROUSAL STATE", "M", "A condition of being stuck in an activated, on-guard mode for long stretches.", "The body can feel always slightly fast, even at rest. Sleep, focus and patience can suffer.", "Irritability, trouble concentrating, poor sleep, and difficulty relaxing even in safe places.", "It is not weakness. It is an understandable response to stress or trauma, and it can ease with care and time.", ""],
  ["HYPERCRITICAL", "R", "Judging yourself or others harshly and often, and finding fault in almost everything.", "Criticism can feel relentless, and good efforts may seem not good enough. Others may feel tense around you.", "Frequent corrections, sharp comments, and difficulty giving praise to others.", "High standards can be healthy. The pattern is when criticism never stops and leaves people discouraged.", ""],
  ["HYPERFOCUS", "T", "Intense, absorbed attention on one task, so that time seems to disappear.", "Absorption can feel satisfying and sharp. Pulling away can feel jarring, and other needs may slip out of view.", "Working for hours without a break, missing calls, and not hearing others at first.", "Hyperfocus is not laziness. It is a common attention pattern that can be seen in ADHD, and reminders and breaks can help.", ""],
  ["HYPERTHYMESIA", "T", "A rare ability to recall almost every day of one's life in vivid detail.", "Vivid memories can bring joy and warmth. Painful memories can also come back in full detail, which can be hard to bear.", "Recalling exact dates, conversations and small details from years ago with ease.", "It is rare and is not a measure of intelligence. Remembering painful events in detail can make healing harder, and support can help.", ""],
  ["HYPERVENTILATION", "B", "Breathing too fast or too deeply, which can cause tingling, dizziness or a light head.", "Breath can feel short, the hands may tingle, and the head may feel light. The sensation can be frightening, though it usually passes.", "Quick, shallow breaths, sighing, chest movement that seems fast, or sudden dizziness.", "It often happens with panic or stress and usually eases with slow breathing. Chest pain, or breathing trouble that does not ease, needs urgent medical care.", ""],
  ["HYPERVIGILANCE", "M", "A constant state of scanning for danger, even when things are safe.", "The body can stay on alert, and relaxing may feel unsafe. Small noises or changes can bring a jolt of fear.", "Checking exits, scanning faces closely, and startling easily at small sounds.", "It often makes sense after past danger or harm. It can ease with safety, rest and support from a trusted professional.", ""],
  ["HYPERVIGILANT", "R", "A person who stays on alert for threats, often after past harm.", "Staying alert can feel exhausting and lonely. Trusting new people may feel risky, even when there is no clear threat.", "Scanning rooms, watching others closely, and reacting strongly to surprises or changes in tone.", "Being watchful often makes sense after past harm. It can ease with time, safety and care from people you trust.", ""],
  ["HYPNOPHILE", "F", "A deep love of sleep, bedtime and rest.", "Sleep can feel calm and restful, a place to escape the noise of the day. Bedtime may be something to look forward to.", "Going to bed early, enjoying naps, and talking often about sleep or dreams.", "Loving sleep is healthy. Sleeping much more than usual, or waking unrefreshed, can signal low mood or a medical problem to discuss with a doctor.", ""],
  ["HYPOCRITE", "R", "A person who holds others to standards they do not follow themselves.", "The person being judged may feel shamed or angry. Trust can wear down when words and actions do not match.", "Criticizing others for what is excused in oneself, and mixed messages about the same behavior.", "Everyone falls short of their values sometimes. The pattern is when double standards are used to control or shame others.", ""],
  ["ABLUTOMANIAC", "V", "A compulsion to wash or bathe over and over, often to ease anxiety.", "Washing can bring short relief from unease, and then the urge returns. Stopping can feel frightening or incomplete.", "Washing hands or body many times a day, long showers, or skin that is dry or cracked from frequent washing.", "Caring about hygiene is healthy. The urge to wash again and again often comes from anxiety, and it can ease with treatment.", ""],
  ["IDENTIFICATION", "T", "Taking on another person's or group's views, style or identity as your own.", "Feeling close to someone or a group can bring belonging and pride. Losing your own view can feel comfortable at first.", "Copying another person's opinions, speech or style, or defending a group as if it were yourself.", "Identifying with others is normal and part of bonding and learning. It becomes a concern when it replaces your own sense of self.", ""],
  ["IDENTITY CRISIS", "M", "A period of confusion about who you are, what you value and where you belong.", "Questions about yourself can feel unsettling and lonely, and also exciting. The ground can seem to shift beneath your feet.", "Frequent changes in goals, style or beliefs, and strong swings in opinions about yourself or others.", "Questioning identity is common, especially during big changes. It can lead to growth when given time, honest reflection and support.", ""],
  ["IDENTITY FRAGMENTATION", "D", "A feeling that the sense of self is split into pieces that do not connect well.", "Parts of you can feel unlinked, and your sense of who you are may shift. It can be unsettling and hard to describe.", "Sudden shifts in mood, style or interests, and uneven feelings about who you are.", "A split sense of self is not a sign of being broken. It is often linked to stress or past harm, and it can improve with careful therapy.", ""],
  ["IDENTITY RECONSTRUCTION", "K", "Rebuilding your sense of self after loss, harm or a major life change.", "Rebuilding can feel uncertain at first. Over time, values and roles may become clearer and more steady.", "Trying new roles or routines, rethinking values, and building new friendships or interests.", "Reconstruction does not mean starting from zero. Your past remains part of you, and it can be woven into a new sense of self.", ""],
  ["IDIOPATHIC HYPERSOMNIA", "B", "Long periods of daytime sleepiness and heavy sleep, with no known cause.", "Sleep can feel heavy and never fully refreshing. Daytime drowsiness can be strong and hard to fight off.", "Long naps, trouble waking up, or dozing at work, in class or while sitting still.", "It is a real medical condition, not laziness. Drowsiness while driving is dangerous, and a sleep specialist can test for it and suggest treatment.", ""],
  ["INSPIRED PURPOSE", "U", "A sense of direction that feels meaningful and energizing.", "A clear purpose can bring energy and steadiness. Daily tasks can feel connected to something that matters.", "Making time for meaningful work or causes, and speaking with conviction about what matters most.", "Purpose does not have to be grand or permanent. Small, meaningful steps can shape a life well.", ""],
  ["ILLNESS ANXIETY", "A", "Persistent worry about having a serious illness, even with little or no sign of one.", "Body sensations can trigger fear fast. Reassurance from doctors may bring short relief before the worry returns.", "Frequent symptom searches, repeated appointments or tests, and asking others again and again if they are sure.", "The fear feels real even when medical tests are normal. Therapy can help break the cycle of checking and reassurance.", ""],
  ["ILLNESS ANXIETY DISORDER", "D", "A long-lasting, intense fear of serious illness that disrupts daily life.", "Fear can feel constant, and it can be hard to trust a clear test result. Life can narrow around health worries.", "Frequent checking, repeated medical visits, and many questions to doctors or online searches about symptoms.", "It is a recognized, treatable condition, and the distress is real even when tests are normal. Cognitive behavioral therapy can help reduce checking and fear.", ""],
  ["IMMORTAL", "T", "A belief that you cannot be harmed, or a strong wish to live forever.", "The belief can bring a sense of power or calm, but it can hide fear underneath. Pressure to prove it can feel strong.", "Taking big risks without concern, dismissing warnings, or saying nothing can hurt them.", "A wish to live long is normal. The belief that nothing can harm you is risky, and it can be checked against real safety.", "If risky choices could cause serious harm, talk with a doctor or therapist soon. If you feel unsafe now, call or text 988 in the US now, and call 911 if you are in danger."],
  ["IMPOSTER", "T", "A feeling that you are a fraud who will soon be found out.", "Praise can feel undeserved, and fear of being exposed can be constant. Each small mistake can feel like proof.", "Overworking to hide errors, playing down achievements, or avoiding new challenges.", "Many capable people feel this way. A feeling of being a fraud is a thought, not proof that you lack skill.", ""],
  ["IMPOSTER SYNDROME", "T", "A pattern of doubting your skills and crediting success to luck or deception.", "Success can feel fragile, and every mistake can seem to confirm the doubt. Relief may be short-lived.", "Overpreparing, struggling to accept praise, and minimizing achievements in front of others.", "Imposter feelings are very common, even among skilled people. The term is not a formal diagnosis, but the feeling can be real and can ease with practice.", ""],
  ["IMPOVERISHED", "M", "Feeling depleted and emptied out, with little energy, meaning or resources left.", "Life can feel thin and bare, as if there is nothing to draw on. Rest may not seem to help enough.", "Withdrawing from projects, low energy, and telling others you have nothing left to give.", "Feeling depleted usually comes from stress, low mood or too little support. It can change with rest, care and connection.", ""],
  ["IMPULSIVE", "R", "A tendency to act quickly, without thinking through the likely consequences.", "Urges can feel sudden and strong. Regret often comes after the action, and it can make the next choice harder.", "Quick decisions, interrupting others, and spending or speaking before pausing.", "Impulsiveness is a common trait, not a lack of care. Pausing, planning and support can help many people act with more control.", ""],
  ["IMPULSIVITY", "T", "The habit of acting before thinking, which can lead to regret or harm.", "Urges can come fast, and pausing can feel hard. Relief from acting quickly is often brief.", "Quick reactions, risky choices, and interrupting conversations before the other person finishes.", "Impulsivity is not a moral failing. It can be linked to ADHD, stress or poor sleep, and practice can build a helpful pause.", ""],
  ["INCOHERENT", "M", "Speech or thinking that is hard to follow, because it jumps or lacks clear links.", "The person may feel confused or unable to find the right words. Others may feel lost trying to follow along.", "Speech that jumps between topics, sentences that do not connect, and trouble staying on one idea.", "Sudden confusion can come from illness, medicine, stress or a mental health condition. It needs a prompt medical check, and it is not a fixed trait.", "Get a medical check soon, since sudden confusion can have physical causes. For support any time, call or text 988 in the US. Please call 911 or go to an emergency room if someone is in danger."],
  ["INCREASED GASTRIC ACID", "B", "More acid in the stomach than usual, which can cause burning and discomfort.", "A burning feeling in the chest or upper stomach can be uncomfortable and worrying. A sour taste may come up after meals.", "Burping, discomfort after meals, a sour taste, or trouble lying flat after eating.", "Stress can make acid symptoms worse, but it is not the only cause. A doctor can check symptoms that keep coming back.", ""],
  ["INCREASED HEART RATE (TACHYCARDIA)", "B", "A heart that beats faster than usual, even when resting.", "The heart can race or pound, sometimes with light-headedness. The feeling can be alarming, even when it passes.", "Flushing, fast breathing, or a visibly rapid pulse in the neck or wrist.", "Stress, caffeine, fear, heat or illness can raise the heart rate for a time. Chest pain, fainting or a rate that stays fast needs urgent medical care.", ""],
  ["INCREASED SALIVATION", "B", "Producing more saliva than usual, which can cause drooling or a wet mouth.", "Extra saliva can feel uncomfortable and make speaking or swallowing harder. Embarrassment about drooling can be strong.", "Frequent swallowing or spitting, drooling during sleep or talks, and a damp pillow in the morning.", "It can come from nausea, reflux, certain medicines or nerve problems. A doctor can find the cause and suggest treatment.", ""],
  ["INDEPENDENT", "U", "Able to think, decide and act for yourself, while still open to support.", "Independence can feel steady and freeing. Making your own choices can bring pride and confidence.", "Making decisions on your own, managing tasks, and asking for help when you need it.", "Independence is not doing everything alone. Healthy independence includes accepting help when it is safe and useful.", ""],
  ["INFANTILIZATION", "C", "Treating an adult like a child by taking over decisions or speaking down to them.", "The person may feel small, unable to trust their own judgment, and ashamed to speak up. Anger and frustration can build.", "Deciding for another adult, using a childish tone, or punishing choices they make on their own.", "Helping someone is not the same as infantilizing them. It becomes harmful when it takes away an adult's choices and dignity.", ""],
  ["INFANTOPHILIA", "F", "A sexual interest in very young children, which is serious and harmful to them.", "These feelings can bring intense shame, fear and confusion. They can be very distressing to carry alone.", "Usually private, with secrecy, shame and fear of asking for help.", "Having these feelings is not the same as acting on them, and getting help early protects children. Any sexual act with a child is abuse, and viewing sexual images of children harms real children.", "Sexual interest in children is serious, and acting on it harms children. Stop It Now offers a free, confidential helpline for people worried about their own thoughts or behavior, or someone else's: 1-888-773-8368. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis, or call 911 if someone is in danger."],
  ["INFILTRATED", "T", "A feeling that outside thoughts, feelings or influences have taken over your mind.", "It can feel as if your mind is no longer private, and the source of thoughts is unclear. The experience can be frightening and confusing.", "Describing thoughts as coming from outside, or seeming unsure where their own thinking begins.", "This can happen with stress, sleep loss, substances or illness, and with some mental health conditions. It deserves prompt care and often improves with treatment.", "Talk with a doctor or mental health team soon. For support any time, call or text 988 in the US. Please call 911 or go to an emergency room if someone is in danger."],
  ["INFINITE SELF-POSSIBILITY", "U", "A sense that your potential for growth and change has no fixed limit.", "This can bring hope and a sense of openness. Possibilities can feel wider than the old story you told yourself.", "Trying new skills, changing routines, and speaking about the future with curiosity.", "Growth is not endless or effortless. Potential grows through steady practice, support and realistic goals.", ""],
  ["INFINITE SELF-WORTH", "U", "A sense that your value as a person does not depend on what you achieve.", "Worth can feel steady, even after failure. Criticism may sting less when it does not define you.", "Speaking to yourself with respect, asking for help without shame, and setting limits calmly.", "Self-worth is not arrogance or a claim that you never make mistakes. It is a steady respect that stays through mistakes.", ""],
  ["INFLUENCED", "T", "Feeling that other people or outside forces are steering your thoughts or choices.", "It can feel as if choices are not fully your own, which can bring confusion or fear. The pull can be hard to explain.", "Quickly changing opinions after talking with others, or describing outside control over thoughts or actions.", "Everyone is shaped by others to some degree, which is normal. When the feeling is strong, sudden or hard to explain, it deserves care from a doctor or therapist.", "Talk with a doctor or mental health team soon. For support any time, call or text 988 in the US. Please call 911 or go to an emergency room if someone is in danger."],
  ["INFORMATION HOARDING", "C", "Keeping useful facts to yourself so that others lack what they need to act.", "The person left out can feel uninformed, anxious and at a disadvantage. Trust can fade when important details are kept back.", "Holding back key facts until the last minute, or sharing details only with favorites.", "Not every person who keeps notes is hoarding information. The pattern matters when facts are withheld to control or shame others.", ""],
  ["INNATE WORTHINESS", "U", "A sense that you deserve respect and care simply because you exist.", "This belief can bring warmth and a steadier footing. It can soften the pull to prove yourself all the time.", "Accepting kindness without apology, and taking up space in conversations and rooms.", "Innate worth is not something you must earn, but it can be hard to feel. It is a belief that can be practiced and grown over time.", ""],
  ["ETERNAL INNER FREEDOM", "U", "A deep sense of freedom inside that stays steady through changing circumstances.", "Inner freedom can feel calm and spacious. Outer pressures may still be real, but they lose some of their grip.", "Choosing responses with care, letting go of what cannot be controlled, and acting from values.", "Inner freedom does not mean having no responsibilities or limits. It is the ability to choose your response within them.", ""],
  ["INNER CALM ANCHOR", "K", "A calming phrase, image or place you return to when stress rises.", "Returning to an anchor can bring steadiness, even for a short time. It can help you come back to the present moment.", "Pausing to breathe, touching a calm object, or repeating a steady phrase during a hard moment.", "An anchor does not remove stress. It gives you a simple, reliable place to return to while the stress passes.", ""],
  ["INNER CHILD WOUNDING", "T", "The idea that early hurts shape how an adult feels and reacts today.", "Old hurts can come back as sudden shame, fear or anger. Adult situations may trigger feelings that seem bigger than the moment.", "Strong reactions to small events, or a pull toward people who repeat old patterns.", "Early experiences matter, and adults can change. 'Inner child' is a metaphor for this, not a formal diagnosis.", ""],
  ["INNER CHILD INTEGRATION", "K", "A practice of acknowledging younger parts of yourself and bringing care to them.", "Being kind to a younger self can bring tenderness, and sometimes grief. Some feelings may surface in unexpected moments.", "Writing a note to a younger self, or speaking kindly to a memory from early life.", "This practice is not about living in the past. It brings care into the present, and it works best with time and patience.", ""],
  ["INNER CHILD REPARENTING", "K", "A practice of giving yourself the comfort, limits and reassurance you may have lacked.", "This can feel awkward at first and then comforting. Old needs may surface, and that can bring sadness or relief.", "Setting gentle limits with yourself, offering comfort after a hard day, and using kind words.", "It does not replace real support from people. It works best alongside therapy, trusted relationships or both.", ""],
  ["INNER CRITIC", "T", "The harsh inner voice that judges your choices, worth and mistakes.", "The voice can feel loud and certain. It can bring shame, fear of failing and a need to hide mistakes.", "Frequent self-blame, harsh talk about errors, and avoiding new tasks for fear of criticism.", "The inner critic is not the whole truth about you. It can be noticed and answered with a fairer, more balanced voice.", ""],
  ["INNER CRITIC TRANSFORMATION", "K", "A practice of changing a harsh inner critic into a voice that is fair and supportive.", "Changing the tone can feel strange at first. Relief can grow as the harshness softens.", "Writing down a harsh thought and rewriting it with honest kindness, then noticing the difference.", "The aim is not to ignore mistakes. It is to take responsibility without cruelty.", ""],
  ["INNOVATOR", "U", "A person who finds new ways to solve problems or make useful things.", "Innovation can feel exciting and a little uncertain. Each new idea can bring a spark of satisfaction.", "Testing new approaches, asking what could work better, and sharing ideas with others.", "Innovation does not need genius or a big budget. Small changes, tried and refined, can be innovative.", ""],
  ["INSECURE", "M", "Feeling unsure of yourself, your place or others' regard, and often doubting it.", "Doubts can feel loud and uneasy. Reassurance may help for a short time, then the doubt returns.", "Seeking reassurance often, worrying about being left out, or avoiding risks that feel uncertain.", "Insecurity is a common feeling, not a fixed trait. It can ease as trust and self-belief grow over time.", ""],
  ["INSECURITY", "M", "A general sense of uncertainty about yourself, other people or the future.", "A background sense of not being safe or good enough can make everyday life tiring. It can flare after criticism or change.", "Comparing yourself often, seeking praise to feel steady, or hesitating before speaking up.", "Feeling insecure does not mean you are weak. It is a feeling that can be understood, and it gradually lessens with support.", ""],
  ["INSERTED", "T", "A feeling that thoughts or urges are placed into your mind by an outside force.", "It can feel frightening, as if the thought is not your own. The experience can be confusing and hard to explain.", "Saying that thoughts belong to someone else, or describing outside control over the mind.", "This is a symptom that can happen with several conditions. It deserves prompt care, and it often improves with treatment.", "Talk with a doctor or mental health team soon. For support any time, call or text 988 in the US. Please call 911 or go to an emergency room if someone is in danger."],
  ["INSPIRED", "M", "Feeling energized and moved by an idea, a person or a purpose.", "Inspiration can bring warmth, energy and a readiness to act. It can make ordinary days feel more alive.", "Starting new projects with enthusiasm, sharing ideas with others, or talking about what moved you.", "Inspiration comes and goes, and it does not have to last to be useful. Steady habits can keep the spark going.", ""],
  ["INSPIRED CREATION", "U", "Making new things from a spark of inspiration and personal meaning.", "Creating can feel alive and purposeful. A fresh idea can bring a rush of hope and pride.", "Sketching, writing, building or cooking with focus, and sharing the work with a few trusted people.", "Inspired work still takes effort and revision. The spark starts the process, and steady practice finishes it.", ""],
  ["INSPIRED CREATIVE FLOW", "U", "A state of absorbed, effortless making, where time seems to fall away.", "Flow can feel light, focused and joyful. Stopping can feel abrupt, and it can take a moment to return to the room.", "Long stretches of quiet focus, forgetting the clock, and reluctance to stop.", "Flow is not a constant state, and it does not need perfect conditions. It often comes after preparation and regular practice.", ""],
  ["INSPIRED VISIONARY CLARITY", "U", "A clear sense of where you want to go and what matters most.", "Clarity can bring calm focus and a sense of direction. Hard choices may feel easier when the goal is clear.", "Writing down long-term goals, saying no to distractions, and making steady choices toward them.", "Clarity is not certainty about every step. It is a steady direction that can change as you learn.", ""],
  ["INSPIRING", "U", "Able to lift others by sharing energy, courage or hope.", "Being inspiring can bring pride and warmth. It can also bring pressure to always be upbeat.", "Encouraging others, telling honest stories of effort, and offering practical help.", "Inspiring people are not always cheerful. Honesty about struggle often inspires more than false cheer.", ""],
  ["INTEGRATED WHOLENESS", "U", "A sense that your values, feelings and actions form one coherent whole.", "Life can feel steadier when parts of you agree. Less energy goes into hiding or splitting yourself.", "Acting the same way across settings, speaking honestly, and keeping commitments.", "Wholeness does not mean having no pain or conflict. It means the pain can sit inside a life that holds together.", ""],
  ["INTEGRATION", "K", "Bringing different parts of your experience into one coherent, workable whole.", "Integration can feel steadier, with less conflict between parts of you. It may come in gradual steps.", "Connecting past and present in honest talk, and acting in line with your values.", "Integration is not perfection. It is a slow process of making different parts of yourself work together.", ""],
  ["INTELLECTUAL DISABILITY", "D", "A lifelong condition affecting learning, reasoning and everyday skills, starting early.", "Learning and daily tasks may take more time and support. Families and people may feel frustration or worry about the future.", "Slower learning, needing support with reading, money or daily tasks, and simpler communication at times.", "It is not a sign of low worth. With the right support, many people learn, work and build close relationships.", ""],
  ["INTELLECTUALIZATION", "T", "A way of pushing feelings away by focusing on logic, analysis or facts.", "Thinking can feel safer than feeling. Emotions often stay underneath and may return later as tension or sudden upset.", "Explaining painful events in technical detail, and turning personal hurt into general theories.", "Thinking carefully is useful. The pattern is when analysis blocks feeling, and noticing the emotion under the thought can help.", ""],
  ["INTELLECTUALIZING", "T", "Discussing a painful topic in abstract terms rather than how it feels.", "Keeping things abstract can feel calmer. It may also leave you feeling distant from your own experience.", "Debating ideas about a personal problem, quoting studies, or avoiding words like 'hurt' and 'scared'.", "Interest in ideas is healthy. It becomes a barrier when it keeps painful truths at a safe distance.", ""],
  ["INTERGENERATIONAL HEALING", "K", "Repairing hurts that have passed through a family or community over generations.", "Healing can bring hope and grief at once, with sorrow for those who came before. It is often slow and uneven.", "Talking openly about family history, breaking old silences, and building new traditions of care.", "It is not a quick fix for past harm. It usually takes many conversations, support and time.", ""],
  ["INTERGENERATIONAL TRAUMA", "D", "Trauma passed from one generation to the next through family life, stress and silence.", "Family patterns may feel familiar, heavy or confusing, even without knowing the full history. Fear or caution may come up for no clear reason.", "Strong fear around certain topics, protective silence, or reactions that echo family stories.", "It is not only a sign of weakness or bad parenting. Awareness, honest talk and support can help break the pattern.", ""],
  ["INTERMITTENT EXPLOSIVE DISORDER", "D", "A condition with sudden outbursts of anger that are out of proportion to the trigger.", "Anger can rise fast and feel out of control. Regret and shame often follow, and both can add to the strain.", "Sudden shouting, throwing objects, or physical outbursts that seem to come from nowhere.", "Anger is normal, but outbursts that hurt others or damage property need treatment. Therapy and medicines can help many people.", ""],
  ["INTERMITTENT REINFORCEMENT", "T", "A pattern where rewards come unpredictably, which makes a behavior hard to stop.", "Unpredictable rewards can feel addictive, and hope for the next good moment keeps you returning. The cycle can feel hard to step away from.", "Staying in a hard situation for brief good moments, or checking repeatedly for a reward.", "Not every mixed experience is a pattern. It is a concern when uneven rewards keep someone stuck in a harmful situation.", "If this happens in a relationship, a counselor or advocate can help you plan next steps. The National Domestic Violence Hotline is 1-800-799-7233 in the US. For crisis support in the US, call or text 988. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis, or call 911 if someone is in danger."],
  ["INTERNALIZED SHAME", "M", "Shame absorbed from others' judgments that now runs quietly from within.", "Shame can feel constant and quiet, as if the flaw is yours alone. It can make you apologize for things that are not your fault.", "Frequent apologies, avoiding attention, and hiding needs or feelings from others.", "Internalized shame is learned, not a fact about your worth. It can soften with honesty, kindness and support from people you trust.", ""],
  ["INTERNET GAMING DISORDER", "D", "A pattern of heavy online gaming that causes loss of control and daily harm.", "Playing can feel absorbing and rewarding. Stopping can bring irritation or restlessness, and time can slip away.", "Gaming late into the night, skipping work or school, and lying about how much time is spent online.", "Gaming is a normal hobby for many people. The concern is loss of control and harm to sleep, work, relationships or health.", ""],
  ["INTEROCEPTIVE AWARENESS", "K", "Noticing signals from inside the body, such as heartbeat, breath and muscle tension.", "Noticing inner signals can feel grounding. Some sensations may feel uncomfortable at first, which is normal.", "Pausing to notice breath, tension or heartbeat, and naming what the body is saying.", "It is not about ignoring discomfort. It helps you respond to body signals sooner and with more care.", ""],
  ["INTERPERSONAL TRAUMA", "D", "Harm caused by people, such as abuse, neglect or betrayal, that shapes how one relates.", "Trust, closeness and safety may feel hard to rely on. Old hurts can come back when someone reminds you of a past harm.", "Avoiding closeness, strong reactions to conflict, or quick withdrawal from people who seem safe.", "Harm by people is never the survivor's fault. Trauma-focused care can help many people rebuild trust and feel safer.", "A trauma-focused therapist can help. If someone is hurting you now, call 911 if you are in danger. The National Domestic Violence Hotline is 1-800-799-7233 in the US. For crisis support in the US, call or text 988."],
  ["INTERROGATION TACTIC", "C", "Using repeated, pressing or confusing questions to force answers and break resistance.", "The person may feel trapped, exhausted and unsure what to say. Fear of saying the wrong thing can grow.", "Repeated questions, shouting, twisting words, or questioning that goes on late into the night.", "Asking questions is not a tactic by itself. The pattern is when questions are used to pressure, confuse or control someone.", ""],
  ["INTIMIDATION", "C", "Using threats, looks, or force to make someone afraid and to control what they do.", "The target may feel afraid, small and unable to speak freely. Their sense of safety can shrink over time.", "Threatening gestures, yelling, blocking exits, or hints of harm to force compliance.", "Strong anger is not always intimidation. The pattern matters when fear is used to control what someone says or does.", ""],
  ["INTROSPECTION DEFENSE", "T", "Looking inward so much that reflection replaces action or hard conversations.", "Looking inward can feel safer than facing others. Problems can stay unresolved, and frustration can build quietly.", "Long self-analysis in place of conversation, or endless reflection instead of a decision or repair.", "Reflection is valuable. It becomes a defense when it keeps you from facing or changing what needs attention.", ""],
  ["INTRUSIVE MEMORIES", "T", "Unwanted memories of painful events that come back suddenly, with strong feelings.", "A memory can flood in as if it is happening again. The body can react with fear, shame or sadness before the mind catches up.", "Sudden distress with no clear trigger, startling easily, or flinching at reminders.", "These memories are a common response to stress or trauma, not a sign of weakness. Trauma-focused therapy can help them lose their hold.", ""],
  ["INTRUSIVE THOUGHTS", "T", "Unwanted thoughts that appear suddenly and feel urgent or distressing.", "The thought can feel alarming and real, which can bring a pull to check or avoid. Relief often comes only briefly.", "Visible distress, pausing mid-task, or avoiding certain places or people.", "Most people have them. Having one does not mean you want it or will act on it.", ""],
  ["INTUITIVE", "U", "Sensing things through a quick inner knowing, often before the facts are clear.", "A gut sense can feel clear and calm. It can also feel uncertain when fear is loud.", "Pausing before a decision, or trusting a first impression and then checking it.", "Intuition is useful, but it can be wrong. When the stakes are high, check it against facts and trusted advice.", ""],
  ["INTUITIVE GUIDANCE", "U", "A sense of direction that comes from listening inwardly and noticing what feels right.", "Guidance can feel like a quiet nudge, calm and steady. It can help you slow down when the mind is racing.", "Pausing before big choices, journaling, or taking small steps that feel aligned with your values.", "Inner guidance is one input, not the only one. It works best alongside facts, advice and your values.", ""],
  ["INVALIDATION", "C", "Dismissing or denying someone's feelings or experiences as if they do not count.", "The person may doubt their feelings and feel unheard, small or confused. Over time they may stop sharing.", "Saying 'you are too sensitive', waving off concerns, or changing the subject after someone shares pain.", "Disagreeing with a feeling is not the same as dismissing it. The pattern matters when feelings are brushed aside again and again.", ""],
  ["INVIGORATED", "M", "Feeling energized, alert and ready to act on what matters.", "Energy can feel bright and steady, with a sense of readiness. It can make a task feel possible.", "Brisk movement, lively talk, and eagerness to start something new.", "Feeling invigorated does not need to be constant. Enjoy it, and pace yourself so the feeling lasts.", ""],
  ["INVOLUNTARY EMOTIONAL CRYING", "B", "Crying that starts suddenly, is hard to stop, and may not match the moment.", "The crying can feel out of your control and bring embarrassment. It can leave you drained afterward.", "Sudden tears in public, or crying that seems out of proportion to what is happening.", "It is not a weakness. It can come from stress, illness, or changes in the brain, and a doctor can help find the cause.", ""],
  ["IRRITABILITY / ANGER OUTBURSTS", "M", "Being easily annoyed, with sudden flashes of anger that pass quickly.", "Small things can feel like a lot, and tension can build quickly. Regret often follows an outburst.", "Snapping at others, a sharp tone, or sudden anger that fades soon after.", "Irritability is a common sign of stress, low mood, pain or poor sleep. Outbursts that hurt others need care and can improve with support.", ""],
  ["ISOLATION", "M", "Feeling cut off from others, even when people are nearby.", "Being alone can feel heavy, and reaching out can feel too hard. Time may blur, and days can feel the same.", "Declining invitations, spending long stretches alone, or dropping contact with friends.", "Needing time alone is healthy. Isolation becomes a risk when it grows and brings more low mood or stress, and support can help you reconnect.", ""],
  ["ISOLATION TACTIC", "C", "Cutting someone off from friends, family or support to make them more dependent.", "The target may feel alone, confused and unable to check their own view. Asking for help can feel risky.", "Discouraging friends or visits, controlling phone use or travel, and criticizing loved ones.", "Wanting time together is normal. The pattern matters when someone is cut off from support on purpose.", ""],
  ["ISOLATOR", "R", "A person who steers others away from friends and family, often to keep control.", "The person being isolated may feel more alone and more reliant on one relationship over time. Their world can slowly get smaller.", "Criticizing friends, creating conflict before visits, or making someone feel guilty for seeing people.", "Some people are private, and some are protective. The pattern matters when contact is limited in order to keep control.", "A counselor, advocate, or trusted person can help you plan next steps. The National Domestic Violence Hotline is 1-800-799-7233 in the US. In an emergency, call 911. For crisis support in the US, call or text 988."],
  ["JAW CLENCHING", "B", "Tightening the jaw and pressing the teeth together, often during stress or sleep.", "The jaw can feel tight, sore or tired, and headaches may follow. Some people notice the habit only when pain starts.", "Tight jaw muscles, clenched teeth during the day, or a bed partner noticing sounds at night.", "It is often linked to stress or sleep, and it is common. A dentist or doctor can suggest a night guard or other helpful steps.", ""],
  ["JAW CLENCHING / TEETH GRINDING", "B", "Grinding or clenching teeth, often at night, which can wear them down and strain the jaw.", "Jaw soreness, tooth sensitivity, or waking with a tight face can be frustrating. Worry about damage can add to stress.", "Worn or chipped teeth, a dentist noting grinding, or sounds of grinding reported at night.", "It is common and is often linked to stress or sleep. A dentist can fit a night guard, and stress support can help.", ""],
  ["JEALOUS", "M", "Feeling fear or resentment that someone else may take a valued person or place.", "Jealousy can bring a hot, sinking feeling, fear of loss and a strong urge to check. It can make trust feel fragile.", "Checking phones, asking repeatedly where someone was, or sulking when others get attention.", "Jealousy is a common feeling. The pattern matters when it leads to control, suspicion or punishment, and that is worth talking through with a counselor.", ""]
];
